menu "Dancefloor LEDs"

    config DANCEFLOOR_ENABLE_VISUALISER
        bool "Enable LED visualiser (FFT + beat detection + strip)"
        default y
        help
            Turn off to test whether the FFT and LED refresh are stealing time
            from audio -- it is the heaviest thing sharing core 1 with playback.

    config DANCEFLOOR_LED_GPIO
        int "WS2812 data GPIO"
        default 18
        depends on DANCEFLOOR_ENABLE_VISUALISER
        help
            Driven as SPI2 MOSI through the GPIO matrix. Needs a 74AHCT125 level
            shifter: 3.3 V data into a 5 V WS2812 is unreliable without one.

    config DANCEFLOOR_LED_COUNT
        int "Number of LEDs"
        default 8
        depends on DANCEFLOOR_ENABLE_VISUALISER
        help
            Total pixels on the one data line, so chained 8-LED sticks add up:
            four of them is 32, not 8.

            8 is the bring-up figure and small enough to run off the devkit's
            5 V rail. The real strips are 1-2 m and need their own supply.

    choice DANCEFLOOR_LED_TYPE
        prompt "Strip model"
        default DANCEFLOOR_LED_TYPE_WS2812
        depends on DANCEFLOOR_ENABLE_VISUALISER
        help
            Wire order differs between models. Getting this wrong swaps colours
            rather than failing, so it looks like a bug in the patterns.

        config DANCEFLOOR_LED_TYPE_WS2812
            bool "WS2812 / WS2812B (5 V, GRB)"

        config DANCEFLOOR_LED_TYPE_WS2811
            bool "WS2811 (12 V, BRG as wired here, 1 IC per 3 LEDs)"

        config DANCEFLOOR_LED_TYPE_SK6812
            bool "SK6812 RGBW (5 V, GRBW)"
    endchoice

    config DANCEFLOOR_LED_BRIGHTNESS
        int "Brightness cap (percent)"
        default 10
        range 1 100
        depends on DANCEFLOOR_ENABLE_VISUALISER
        help
            Scales every pixel before it is sent. LEDs are the largest single
            load in the power budget -- ~150 pixels at full white is ~45 W --
            and eyes are dark-adapted outdoors at night, so a low figure looks
            no dimmer than a high one and reclaims most of that.

            40 was the original design figure and was too bright on the bench.
            Below about 10 the 8-bit colour resolution starts to show: the
            brightest component becomes 25, so hue steps get visibly coarse and
            the dim tail of the beat envelope quantises to black.

endmenu
